        "U3"
      ],
      "text": "Hand-only loses the present preference test: the calculated rival completes the same fixed demand with seven versus nine occupied transfer-clerk minutes. A hypothetical future escape cannot reverse that ranking. Neither sequence is an observed trial, an empirical distribution, or a supplied global-optimum proof."
    },
    {
      "id": "DEV-R09-stage2-adaptation-limit",
      "source_refs": [
        "O7",
        "U2",
        "R6",
        "R7",
        "M1",
        "M2",
        "M3",
        "M4"
      ],
      "text": "Rival backcasts remain hypotheses: hand substitution could preserve unchanged later requests and escape trolley dependence; routine trolley exposure could induce smaller, more frequent later requests and recreate dependence. The specific service-to-request adaptation is unobserved. Present controls and ledger observables contain no later request-size or frequency measure, so no admissible present probe separates these worlds. Matched future exposure with unchanged requests would weaken adaptation; fragmentation after exposure could support it, but neither evidence nor that experiment is supplied."
    },
    {
      "id": "DEV-R09-stage2-anomaly",
      "source_refs": [
        "O6",
        "C6",
        "O5",
        "U1",
        "O8"
      ],
      "text": "Both schedules retain B9 counting, spare-bracket addition, recounting and sealing for two minutes. Its ten-bracket mark conceals nine brackets and proves neither correctness nor release. Its cause is unknown and implies no further failure. Current quantities and durations are synthetic stipulations."
    }
  ],
